      {
        "kind": "text",
        "heading": "The rules of differentiation",
        "body": {
          "en": "We rarely use the limit definition directly. These rules build every elementary derivative:",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Power rule",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\dfrac{d}{dx}x^{n}=n\\,x^{\\,n-1} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Constant multiple",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\dfrac{d}{dx}\\big(c\\,f\\big)=c\\,f' \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Sum",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( (f+g)'=f'+g' \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Product",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( (fg)'=f'g+fg' \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Quotient",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\Big(\\dfrac{f}{g}\\Big)'=\\dfrac{f'g-fg'}{g^{2}} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Chain",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\dfrac{d}{dx}f\\big(g(x)\\big)=f'\\!\\big(g(x)\\big)\\,g'(x) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Reference — Common derivatives",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>\\( \\dfrac{d}{dx}\\sin x=\\cos x \\), \\(\\quad \\dfrac{d}{dx}\\cos x=-\\sin x \\), \\(\\quad \\dfrac{d}{dx}\\tan x=\\sec^2 x \\),</p><p>\\( \\dfrac{d}{dx}e^{x}=e^{x} \\), \\(\\quad \\dfrac{d}{dx}\\ln x=\\dfrac1x \\quad (x&gt;0). \\)</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 2.A — Chain rule",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Differentiate \\( y=\\sin\\!\\big(3x^2\\big) \\).</p><p>Outer function \\( \\sin u \\) with \\( u=3x^2 \\). Then \\( \\dfrac{dy}{du}=\\cos u \\) and \\( \\dfrac{du}{dx}=6x \\). By the chain rule,</p>\\[ \\frac{dy}{dx}=\\cos\\!\\big(3x^2\\big)\\cdot 6x = 6x\\cos\\!\\big(3x^2\\big). \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 2.B — Product rule",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Differentiate \\( y=x^2 e^{x} \\).</p><p>With \\( f=x^2 \\) (so \\( f'=2x \\)) and \\( g=e^x \\) (so \\( g'=e^x \\)):</p>\\[ y'=2x\\,e^{x}+x^2 e^{x}=x e^{x}(x+2). \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "figure",
        "caption": {
          "en": "Tangent line \\(y = 2x - 1\\) to parabola \\(y = x^2\\) at point of tangency \\(P(1, 1)\\), demonstrating gradient \\(m = f'(1) = 2\\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "figure": {
          "type": "fn",
          "fns": [
            { "fn": "x^2", "label": "y = x^2" },
            { "fn": "2*x - 1", "label": "Tangent: y = 2x - 1" }
          ],
          "points": [
            { "x": 1, "y": 1, "label": "P(1, 1)" }
          ],
          "xrange": [-2, 3],
          "yrange": [-2, 5]
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "warn",
        "label": {
          "en": "⚠ Common Traps & Exam Pitfalls",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<strong>Product Rule Trap:</strong> The derivative of a product is NOT the product of derivatives: \\((uv)' \\ne u'v'\\). Always use \\((uv)' = u'v + uv'\\).<br><br><strong>Quotient Rule Order:</strong> Order matters in subtraction: \\(\\left(\\frac{u}{v}\\right)' = \\frac{u'v - uv'}{v^2}\\) (the numerator is \\(u'v - uv'\\), never reversed).<br><br><strong>Chain Rule Inner Function:</strong> When differentiating composite functions such as \\([g(x)]^n\\), \\(e^{g(x)}\\), or \\(\\sin(g(x))\\), never forget to multiply by the derivative of the inside: \\(g'(x)\\). For example, \\(\\frac{d}{dx}\\cos(5x) = -5\\sin(5x)\\), not \\(-\\sin(5x)\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "Find the equation of the tangent line to the curve \\(y = x^3 - 4x + 1\\) at the point where \\(x = 2\\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "At \\(x = 2\\), \\(y = 2^3 - 4(2) + 1 = 8 - 8 + 1 = 1\\), so the point is \\((2, 1)\\). Differentiating gives \\(\\frac{dy}{dx} = 3x^2 - 4\\). At \\(x = 2\\), the slope is \\(m = 3(4) - 4 = 8\\). Using point-slope form: \\(y - 1 = 8(x - 2) \\implies y = 8x - 15\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
